# Each of these spawns a real `node` and asserts on what the child did. The
# child is node running node's own `async_hooks`, so the assertion is about
# node's binary; there is no way to install our module into a process we did
# not start.
#
# The three stack-overflow files are worth naming precisely, because the thing
# they test is real and we will want it later: that a `RangeError` from an
# exhausted stack still reaches `uncaughtException` when hooks are enabled,
# rather than aborting. It is untestable from here only because the overflow
# has to happen in a process the test controls from the outside.
test-async-hooks-fatal-error.js: spawns node to watch a throwing hook abort the child
test-async-hooks-stack-overflow.js: spawns node to overflow its stack with hooks enabled
test-async-hooks-stack-overflow-nested-async.js: spawns node, same, from nested async frames
test-async-hooks-stack-overflow-try-catch.js: spawns node, same, inside a try/catch

# These four run the whole test inside a `worker_threads` Worker, which is
# node's thread running node's `async_hooks` from an `eval` string. Our module
# is never loaded in there. They pass with this module blanked, which is what
# gave them away.
test-async-hooks-worker-asyncfn-terminate-1.js: runs in a node Worker, on node's async_hooks
test-async-hooks-worker-asyncfn-terminate-2.js: runs in a node Worker, on node's async_hooks
test-async-hooks-worker-asyncfn-terminate-3.js: runs in a node Worker, on node's async_hooks
test-async-hooks-worker-asyncfn-terminate-4.js: runs in a node Worker, on node's async_hooks
